WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
For the course’s more common DDK method, measure the

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.300
seconds needed for a specified number of repetitions.

3
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.740
Instruct and model, say go and start timing, then

4
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.000
stop after twenty repetitions of a single syllable.

5
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.180
After examining syllables separately, time ten repetitions of puh tuh kuh.

6
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.340
Record the conditions and findings.

7
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.400
The alternate method counts syllables produced within a fixed time.

8
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.900
The current Week four slide specifies fifty to one hundred minimum utterances.

9
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.260
Build rapport, minimize interruptions, wait, and use topics

10
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.840
that elicit more than names or yes-no answers.

11
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:41.140
Vary activities, contexts and partners when possible.

12
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.740
Pictures with actions, personal events and narrative retelling provide connected language.

13
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.580
Reading supplies fixed words that cannot be avoided, but is

14
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.920
appropriate only for a client who can read the material.

15
00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.740
Record at least one to two minutes of

16
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.060
connected speech for the course rate procedure.

17
00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.040
Time the sample in seconds, count its words,

18
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.726
divide words by seconds and multiply by sixty.

19
00:01:02.746 --> 00:01:07.240
Sixty words in seventy-five seconds gives forty-eight words per minute.

20
00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.000
The clinical question is how rate affects this person’s communication.

21
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.760
A norm alone cannot describe that effect.

22
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.900
Intelligibility is how well a listener understands speech.

23
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.080
Error type and frequency matter, but so do listener

24
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.980
familiarity, context, face-to-face contact, nonverbal cues and environment.

25
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.400
Transcribe a representative sample and calculate words understood

26
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.480
divided by total words, times one hundred.

27
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.535
Record the conditions.

28
00:01:33.555 --> 00:01:36.966
Eighty understood words out of one hundred is

29
00:01:36.986 --> 00:01:41.135
eighty percent intelligibility, not an articulation percentile rank.
